The handoff document contains a complete, verified counterexample. I'll write the proof to proof.md.The proof has been written to `proof.md`. 

**Summary of the answer:** $\boxed{\text{No}}$ — the complete intersection property of one nonzero fiber does not force all nonzero fibers to be complete intersections.

The counterexample is $X = \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{C}[x,y,t]/(x^2 - t(t-1)y,\, xy,\, y^2)) \to \mathbb{A}^1_{\mathbb{C}}$, which is flat (free rank-3 $\mathbb{C}[t]$-module by Gröbner basis). The fiber at $t=2$ is the complete intersection $(y - x^2/2,\, x^3)$, while the fiber at $t=1$ is $\mathfrak{m}^2 = (x^2, xy, y^2)$, which has codimension 2 but requires 3 generators, so it is not a complete intersection. Both $c_1 = 2$ and $c_2 = 1$ are nonzero.
